## DESCRIPTION
## Calculate the remainder of a large number using divisibility tests.
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Number theory)
## DBchapter(Divisibility)
## DBsection(Divisibility Tests)
## Institution(Fitchburg State University)
## Author(Peter Staab)
## MO(1)

# updated by Peter Staab, Fitchburg State University, 2022.10.31

############################
# Initialization

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

$num = random(5000000, 99999999);

$ans1 = Compute($num % 2);
$ans2 = Compute($num % 4);
$ans3 = Compute($num % 5);
$ans4 = Compute($num % 8);
$ans5 = Compute($num % 10);

############################
# Main Text

BEGIN_PGML

a) What is the remainder of [$num] divided by 2? [___]{$ans1}
b) What is the remainder of [$num] divided by 4? [___]{$ans2}
c) What is the remainder of [$num] divided by 5? [___]{$ans3}
d) What is the remainder of [$num] divided by 8? [___]{$ans4}
e) What is the remainder of [$num] divided by 10? [___]{$ans5}

*Hint*: Think of divisibility tests

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

a) just look at the last digit.  If odd, then dividing by 2 results in 1 or else 0.  In this case, the answer is [$ans1].
b) look at the last two digits.  This is how the divisibility by 4 works.  In this case we need to compute [@ [$num] %100 @] [` \div 4 `] which is just [$ans2].
c) we again just look at the last two digits and divide by 5.  This is [$ans3].
d) we need to just divide the last 3 digits or [@ [$num] %1000 @] by 8.  The result is [$ans4].
e) dividing by 10 is the same as looking at the last digit or [$ans5].

END_PGML_SOLUTION
ENDDOCUMENT();
